summary:
- topic: Superintendent Transition
  text: The board approved a separation agreement with Superintendent Metheny, effective
    August 8, 2025, and appointed Dr. George Entwistle as the Interim Superintendent
    for the 2025-2026 school year.
- topic: Board Leadership
  text: Following a nomination and unanimous vote, Tandy Ratliff was elected as the
    new Chair of the South Portland Board of Education.
- topic: Personnel
  text: The district is in a strong hiring position for the upcoming school year,
    with less than 2% of teaching positions and only 10 full-time ed tech positions
    vacant. New principals for Small Elementary and an assistant principal for SPHS
    were among the approved hires.
- topic: Facilities and Security
  text: Major summer facilities projects are underway, including the installation
    of new surveillance camera systems and secure vestibules at all five elementary
    schools, and roofing work at Small, Kaler, and Skillin schools.
- topic: Artificial Intelligence
  text: The Director of Technology presented the district's proactive approach to
    AI, including an AI Steering Committee, the vetted 'Magic School' tool, and plans
    for student and staff education using an 'AI Traffic Light' protocol to guide
    usage.
- topic: Budget and Finance
  text: A preliminary review of the FY2024-25 budget indicates a potential deficit,
    with overages in school administration, program function, and transportation.
    The finance committee will conduct a deeper analysis.
- topic: Superintendent's Farewell
  text: In his final report, Superintendent Metheny thanked the board and community,
    highlighted district achievements like improved SAT scores and reduced chronic
    absenteeism, and noted future challenges including federal funding uncertainty
    and the societal impact of AI.
- topic: Student and Community Programs
  text: The student representative reported on the success of the summer meals program,
    extended school year for special education, and various summer camps. The district
    is also partnering with the Maine Mall for a 'Fill the Backpack' supply drive.
